• #1219 (closed): Commit 2b7b4a7d fixed it. --output-format stream-json -o <file> now writes the final answer under the same rules as the other modes. It replaces the file on success and leaves it alone if the run fails or is interrupted. While fixing it I found that Ctrl+C in stream-json mode didn't set the interrupt flag. So once -o worked, a partial reply would have replaced the file and the run would still have exited 0. The run now keeps the file and exits 130. My closing comment also says what's still missing: under stream-json, SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGQUIT end the process straight away. The file is left untouched, but nothing on stderr says so. I read that from the code and didn't run it, and the docs already mention it.
